## 🚀 Exploitation / Solution Path
Detail your step-by-step breakdown to get the flag:

1. Analysis & Identification

• The challenge text retains standard English word spacing, sentence structure, and punctuation, indicating a Monoalphabetic Substitution Cipher.
• The flag trailing at the end follows a standard format: lrlxpac{...}.

2. Finding the Cribs (Known Plaintext)

• The Introduction: The phrase RZFt (tquvz fuv rlwzbvp zqp felg) maps structurally to the standard security acronym definition: CTFs (short for capture the flag).
	• This yields the baseline mappings: R → C, Z → T, F → F, t → s, l → a, v → r, p → e.
• The Flag Format: The prefix lrlxpac has identical characters at positions 1, 3, and 7 (l). Matching this against the target word academy confirms the mapping rule where ciphertext l/L translates to plaintext a/A.

3. Decoding the Flag Segment

Applying the discovered character mapping rules to the ciphertext string inside the brackets (FV3DB3ORC_4774RM5_4V3_R001_0532L335) resolves the leetspeak text:
• FV3DB3ORC → FR3QU3NCY
• 4774RM5 → 4774CK5
• 4V3 → 4R3
• R001 → C001
• 0532L335 → 0532A335 (Crucially, uppercase L translates to A)

4. Flag 
academy{FR3QU3NCY_4774CK5_4R3_C001_0532A335}

5. For you the flag may vary at last.

